  <h1>State Spousal Support Guidelines</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Harper Quinn (HQ), Editor-in-Chief &amp; Family Law Lead. Updated May 2026.</em></p>

  <p>Spousal support law varies dramatically by state. The same fact pattern &mdash; a 15-year marriage, $80,000 income gap, one homemaker spouse &mdash; can produce very different outcomes depending on where the divorce is filed. Below are the notable approaches by state, organized by framework type.</p>

  <h2>Formula-based states for temporary or permanent support</h2>

  <h3>California</h3>
  <p>California uses local court formulas for temporary (pendente lite) support during divorce proceedings. The most widely cited is the Santa Clara County formula: 40% of the higher-earning spouse&rsquo;s net monthly income minus 50% of the lower-earning spouse&rsquo;s net monthly income. Other counties use variations; the DissoMaster and X-PERT software programs are commonly used to calculate temporary support amounts in California courts.</p>
  <p>Permanent support in California is entirely judicial discretion under Family Code &sect; 4320 factors. There is no binding formula for the final permanent award. Courts consider all 14 statutory factors including the standard of living, earning capacity, contributions to the marriage, and the goal of self-sufficiency. California is a strong state for supported spouses in long marriages: marriages of 10 or more years create a rebuttable presumption that the court will retain jurisdiction over support indefinitely.</p>

  <h3>Massachusetts</h3>
  <p>The Massachusetts Alimony Reform Act of 2011 created a comprehensive statutory framework. The general term formula: 30&ndash;35% of the difference between the parties&rsquo; gross incomes. Duration is tied to marriage length by statute: marriages under 5 years yield support for up to 50% of the marriage length; 5&ndash;10 years, up to 60%; 10&ndash;15 years, up to 70%; 15&ndash;20 years, up to 80%. Marriages of 20 or more years may produce indefinite support, though the statute created a presumption that support ends when the payor reaches full Social Security retirement age. Massachusetts also defines four alimony types: general term, rehabilitative, reimbursement, and transitional.</p>

  <h3>Pennsylvania</h3>
  <p>Pennsylvania uses a formula modeled on its child support guidelines for spousal support and alimony pendente lite. The basic formula: 33% of the difference in the parties&rsquo; net monthly incomes when no dependent children are involved; adjusted downward when child support is also ordered to prevent double-counting household resources. Permanent alimony (called &ldquo;alimony&rdquo; post-divorce in Pennsylvania) is determined by multi-factor analysis without a binding formula.</p>

  <h3>Texas</h3>
  <p>Texas calls spousal support &ldquo;maintenance&rdquo; and imposes strict statutory caps. Amount: the lesser of $5,000/month or 20% of the supporting spouse&rsquo;s average monthly gross income. Duration: 5 years for marriages of 10&ndash;20 years; 7 years for marriages of 20&ndash;30 years; 10 years for marriages of 30+ years. Disability-based maintenance may be indefinite. Eligibility requires at least 10 years of marriage (with narrow exceptions for family violence convictions or disability). Texas is among the most restrictive states for spousal support: the caps and duration limits significantly constrain recovery even in long marriages with large income gaps.</p>

  <h3>Illinois</h3>
  <p>Illinois uses a statutory formula for maintenance when combined gross income is $500,000 or less: 33.3% of the payer&rsquo;s net annual income minus 25% of the recipient&rsquo;s net annual income, with the total not exceeding 40% of combined net income. Duration is calculated as a percentage of marriage length: 20% for a 3-year marriage, increasing to 80% for a 15-year marriage. Marriages of 20+ years may produce indefinite maintenance at judicial discretion. Above $500,000 combined income, the formula is discretionary rather than mandatory.</p>

  <h3>New York</h3>
  <p>New York uses the Maintenance Guidelines Act formula for temporary (pendente lite) support: 30% of the payor&rsquo;s income minus 20% of the payee&rsquo;s income (when the payee does not have the children), subject to income caps (approximately $203,000 in guideline income). Post-divorce maintenance applies similar formulas for the final order but is subject to the court&rsquo;s discretion to adjust based on the equitable factors in Domestic Relations Law &sect; 236. Duration follows guidelines tied to marriage length with the court retaining discretion to extend.</p>

  <h2>Discretion-heavy states</h2>
  <p>Most states use multi-factor analysis without a binding statutory formula for permanent support. Outcomes in these states vary significantly by judge and county. Notable examples:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Florida (post-2023):</strong> Florida&rsquo;s 2023 alimony reform (effective July 1, 2023) abolished permanent alimony. New divorces in Florida may only include bridge-the-gap alimony (up to 2 years), rehabilitative alimony (with a specific plan), durational alimony (capped at 50% of marriage length for short marriages, 60% for moderate, 75% for long), or temporary alimony during proceedings. This was a major change for long-marriage spouses who previously had access to permanent alimony in Florida.</li>
    <li><strong>Georgia:</strong> Multi-factor discretionary analysis. Courts consider need and ability to pay as primary factors, with significant weight to contributions to marriage and standard of living.</li>
    <li><strong>Arizona:</strong> Arizona has formal maintenance guidelines that are advisory but not binding. Courts apply multi-factor analysis under A.R.S. &sect; 25-319. Duration is typically 1/3 to 1/2 of marriage length for moderate-length marriages.</li>
    <li><strong>Colorado:</strong> Colorado revised its maintenance guidelines in 2014 to introduce advisory formula guidelines: 40% of the higher income minus 50% of the lower income (capped at 40% of combined income). Duration guidelines range from 11% of marriage length for a 3-year marriage to 50% for a 20-year marriage. Guidelines are advisory; courts retain full discretion.</li>
    <li><strong>Ohio:</strong> Multi-factor discretionary analysis under O.R.C. &sect; 3105.18. Courts consider 14 statutory factors. No binding formula exists; outcomes vary significantly across Ohio counties.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>States with notable caps or recent changes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Texas:</strong> $5,000/month or 20% of gross income cap on amount; 5-year duration cap for most marriages (see above).</li>
    <li><strong>Florida:</strong> Permanent alimony abolished effective July 1, 2023; replaced with durational support capped at percentage of marriage length.</li>
    <li><strong>Massachusetts:</strong> Support duration capped by percentage of marriage length; presumptive termination when payor reaches full Social Security retirement age.</li>
    <li><strong>New Jersey:</strong> 2014 alimony reform limited &ldquo;permanent&rdquo; alimony and created a rebuttable presumption that open-duration alimony terminates when the payor reaches full retirement age. Cohabitation can trigger a modification hearing.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Practical implication: where you file matters enormously</h2>
  <p>Jurisdiction selection — which state&rsquo;s courts have jurisdiction over your divorce — can significantly affect spousal support outcomes when spouses have connections to more than one state. A 15-year marriage with an $80,000 income gap filed in California (potentially indefinite permanent support at full standard of living) produces a very different result than the same marriage filed in Texas ($5,000/month cap, 5-year duration cap). Forum selection is a legitimate strategic consideration in divorces where both spouses have residential ties to multiple states. Consult a family-law attorney in the relevant states before assuming which state will govern your case.</p>

